Reasoning through the schema:

Distinct entity types. Each row is one conflict event, so the central entity is the event itself, which fits bsm:Action (it has event start/end times, an action description, a classification code, involved actors, a geographic location, and results). actor1 and actor2 are real-world parties (e.g. "Military Forces of Poland (2023-)", "Unidentified Armed Group (Baltic)"), and Action has the object property bsm:involvedActor leading to bsm:Actor, so each actor is its own entity, not a literal on the event. I use bsm:Actor rather than bsm:Organisation because ACLED actors can also be crowds like "Rioters" or "Civilians", which are not organisations. The location columns support a bsm:Location entity (reached from Action via bsm:hasGeographicLocation), and latitude/longitude support a bsm:AbsolutePoint reached via bsm:hasGeometry — that linked Geometry class is one hop away and easy to miss since its columns sit on the same row. Because Battlespace Concept offers bsm:partOf, the admin hierarchy (location → admin1 → country → region) can be modeled as nested Location entities; I stop at region and leave admin2/admin3 out of the chain because they can be blank and would silently break links. fatalities supports a bsm:ActionEffect entity (Action → bsm:hasResult → ActionEffect with bsm:effectQuantityNumber). The iso column is named for the scheme itself (ISO 3166 per its description) and carries the code value, which is exactly the structure bsm:AlternateIdentifier models (standard code + value), so I create it hanging off the country Location via bsm:hasAlternateIdentifier.

Identifiers. Action: event_{event_id_cnty} (event_id_cnty is unique and stable). Actors: actor_{actor1} and actor_{actor2} (names are the identity; actor2 may be blank, in which case that entity is skipped, which is correct). Event location: location_{country}_{admin1}_{location} (all three are always populated; admin2/admin3 are excluded because blanks would drop the entity). Admin1: location_{country}_{admin1}; country: location_{country}; region: location_region_{region}. Point: point_{latitude}_{longitude}. Effect: effect_{event_id_cnty}. Alternate identifier: altid_iso_{iso}.

Property decisions and near-synonym checks. event_date maps to bsm:eventStartDateTime; the ontology splits start vs end and the column ("the date on which the event took place") does not say which, so this is genuinely ambiguous — I map to start as the occurrence date and flag it. notes maps to bsm:actionDescriptionText ("a short description of the event" vs "action description" — strong match). For the type columns there are two near-synonym candidates, bsm:classificationCode and bsm:conceptName, and two competing columns, event_type and disorder_type; I assign event_type → bsm:classificationCode (the primary "type of event" classification) and leave disorder_type unmapped rather than guessing a second home for it, noting the "code" qualifier is only loosely satisfied by text values. sub_event_type "further specifies the nature of the event", so it goes to bsm:additionalInformationText on the Action at low confidence. tags ("additional structured information about the event") also fits bsm:additionalInformationText; since I avoid doubling the same property on one subject, tags wins that slot on the strength of its description and civilian_targeting is left unmapped. event_id_cnty → bsm:sourceIdentifier: the description ("unique alphanumeric event identifier... remains constant") matches the source-system identifier reading better than the source column does, so the source column itself is left unmapped. On the actors: actor name → bsm:conceptName, inter1/inter2 → bsm:typeName ("a text value indicating the type of Actor 1" vs "type name"), and assoc_actor_1/2 → bsm:alternateName, since the description says they are "actor designations that further identify" the main actor (moderate-low confidence; they can also be separate co-actors, but no clean property links those). On Location: location/admin1/country/region each → bsm:locationName on their own entity, chained by bsm:partOf. On AbsolutePoint: latitude → bsm:latitudeDecimal and longitude → bsm:longitudeDecimal (exact name and EPSG:4326/WGS84 match). On ActionEffect: fatalities → bsm:effectQuantityNumber ("number of reported fatalities" vs "effect quantity"), plus a constant bsm:effectDescriptionCode of "fatalities" at low confidence. On AlternateIdentifier: constant bsm:alternateIdentifierStandardCode "ISO 3166" and bsm:alternateIdentifierValue ← iso.

Unmapped: year, time_precision, disorder_type, interaction, civilian_targeting, admin2, admin3, geo_precision, source, source_scale, timestamp, population_1km, population_2km, population_5km, population_best — none have a suitable ontology term.

IRI consistency check: every iri-typed value's transformation string is copied character-for-character from the referenced entity's subject_transformation (actor_{actor1}, actor_{actor2}, location_{country}_{admin1}_{location}, location_{country}_{admin1}, location_{country}, location_region_{region}, point_{latitude}_{longitude}, effect_{event_id_cnty}, altid_iso_{iso}). No transformation keys are emitted as empty objects, and all class/property URIs appear verbatim in the ontology.

